# ALLSEER line-ending policy.
#
# Deliberately narrow. Everything not named here keeps falling back to the
# developer's core.autocrlf, which is the behavior this repository already had
# and which is fine for source: Go files are read by a compiler that does not
# care, and gofmt-clean is asserted after normalizing line endings.
#
# The golden decision streams are different in kind. They are not source, they
# are **committed output**: the exact bytes internal/audit.JSONLSink produces
# for a replayed session, compared byte for byte by test/golden. The sink emits
# LF and nothing else — one decision per line, terminated with "\n" by
# encoding/json's Encoder — because the audit format is a wire contract with
# external tooling, and "one JSON object per line" has to mean the same thing on
# every host that reads the log.
#
# Left to core.autocrlf, a Windows checkout smudges these files to CRLF and the
# golden test fails on a fresh clone: the bytes on disk stop matching the bytes
# the writer produces. That is the test correctly reporting a real divergence,
# so the fix belongs here rather than in the test or in the sink.
#
# `text eol=lf` rather than `-text`, for two reasons:
#
#   - It is self-healing. An editor or a tool that rewrites one of these files
#     with CRLF has the change normalized back to LF on the way into the index,
#     so the canonical form cannot be lost by accident. `-text` would commit
#     whatever bytes happened to be on disk and break every other platform.
#   - It keeps them diffable. `-text` marks a file binary for diff purposes, and
#     `git diff -- test/testdata/golden/` reporting "Binary files differ" would
#     destroy the entire review workflow these files exist for: a golden that
#     changes is a change in what the system concludes about a session, and a
#     human has to read that diff and approve it.
test/testdata/golden/*.jsonl text eol=lf
